<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Measuring Relative Confidence</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we elaborate on the algorithms we use to convert pairwise confidence
preferences into scalar relative confidence scores for each question.
For every pair of questions <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we prompt the model to indicate
which it is more confident in answering to produce a set <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of pairwise
preferences <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}&lt;q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}&lt;q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Below, we present the details of the ELO and TrueSkill methods to convert these
preferences to scalar confidence scores.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Confidence Estimation Using Elo Rating</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We treat each question as a “player” in a tournament, where each pairwise preference is interpreted as a match outcome. All questions are initialized with the same Elo score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elo and Sloan [1978]</a></cite> (in our case, 1000). For each preference pair <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}&lt;q_{j}\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}&lt;q_{j}\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the preferred (winning) question and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the less preferred (losing) question, we compute the expected win probability using the logistic function:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(i\text{ wins})=\frac{1}{1+10^{(S_{j}-S_{i})/K}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> wins</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(i\text{ wins})=\frac{1}{1+10^{(S_{j}-S_{i})/K}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the Elo scores of questions <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a sensitivity factor. The scores are then updated as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\leftarrow S_{i}+K\cdot(1-P(i\text{ wins}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> wins</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\leftarrow S_{i}+K\cdot(1-P(i\text{ wins}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}\leftarrow S_{j}-K\cdot P(i\text{ wins})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> wins</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}\leftarrow S_{j}-K\cdot P(i\text{ wins})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This update process is repeated for all preference pairs over multiple iterations to allow scores to converge. The final Elo scores are normalized using min-max scaling to the range <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,100]</annotation></semantics></math> to produce interpretable confidence scores:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Confidence}(q_{i})=\frac{S_{i}-\min(S)}{\max(S)-\min(S)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Confidence</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Confidence}(q_{i})=\frac{S_{i}-\min(S)}{\max(S)-\min(S)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Confidence Estimation Using TrueSkill</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As an alternative to Elo, we also implement confidence estimation using the TrueSkill rating system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herbrich et al. [2006]</a></cite>, which models each question’s confidence as a Gaussian distribution over skill: <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> represents the estimated confidence and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> the uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each preference pair <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}&lt;q_{j}\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}&lt;q_{j}\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we update the distributions of both questions using Bayesian inference. The update is performed using the TrueSkill factor graph model, which adjusts both <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> based on the observed outcome and the prior distributions. After processing all preference pairs, we extract the mean <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of each question’s distribution as its raw confidence score. These scores are then min-max normalized to the range <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,100]</annotation></semantics></math> as in the Elo method.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This methodology enables robust and interpretable confidence estimation by
leveraging the model’s relative preferences, rather than relying on coarse,
absolute confidence scores.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We create the MCQA problems for the codenet tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al. [2021]</a></cite> covering (1) code generation; (2) code understanding; (3) code syntax; and (4) code repair. For doing this scalably we
Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.3 Dataset ‣ Appendix A Measuring Relative Confidence ‣ Do Code Models Suffer from the Dunning-Kruger Effect?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> summarizes the number of tasks used for each domain.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Data Statistics</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T6.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T6.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T6.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Domain</span></th>
<th id="A1.T6.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T6.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Number of Samples</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T6.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T6.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ada</span></th>
<td id="A1.T6.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T6.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">118</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T6.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A1.T6.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bash</span></th>
<td id="A1.T6.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="A1.T6.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1000</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T6.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A1.T6.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">C</span></th>
<td id="A1.T6.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="A1.T6.5.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">897</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T6.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A1.T6.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">C#</span></th>
<td id="A1.T6.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="A1.T6.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1000</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T6.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A1.T6.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">C++</span></th>
